kernelsu-dkms for Debian
========================

This package ships the KernelSU kernel module as a DKMS source tree in
/usr/src/kernelsu-<version>. DKMS rebuilds the module whenever a new kernel
or new kernel headers are installed.

Getting the module built
------------------------
The postinst builds the module for every installed kernel that has matching
headers under /lib/modules/<kver>/build, not just the running kernel. A
kernel without headers is skipped; to cover it, install its headers and run:

    sudo apt install linux-headers-<kver>
    sudo dkms autoinstall

dkms.conf sets AUTOINSTALL="yes", so DKMS also builds the module
automatically when new kernel headers are installed later. Check the result
with:

    dkms status -m kernelsu

Loading the module
------------------
Plain "modprobe kernelsu" cannot load this module: it references kernel
symbols (kallsyms_lookup_name, path_mount, ...) that stock kernels do not
export, and the in-kernel module loader can only bind exported symbols.
Attempts end with a flood of "Unknown symbol ... (err -2)" in dmesg.

The module is instead loaded by a userspace loader that relocates those
symbols against /proc/kallsyms and then calls init_module. For it, the
package postinst also publishes an uncompressed copy of every built module
as /lib/modules/<kver>/extra/kernelsu.ko. Any of the following loads the
module (as root):

    sudo kernelsu-loader          # ships with this package, no extra deps
    sudo load-kernelsu            # uses "modloader" when installed, and
                                  # falls back to kernelsu-loader
    # or simply start Waydroid: its in-container ksuinit loads the module
    # from that same path.

Verify with "lsmod | grep kernelsu" and the KernelSU messages in dmesg.
"sudo modprobe kernelsu" is expected to fail and is not an error of the
build.

GXDE 6.6 kernels backport anon_inode_create_getfile (mainline 6.8) and the
four-argument change_pid (mainline 6.15). The DKMS sources installed by this
package have the matching version gates lowered so those kernels build
correctly. On a stock mainline kernel without these backports the build can
still fail; the postinst reports such kernels without aborting installation.

Installed files
---------------
  /usr/src/kernelsu-<version>/         DKMS module sources and dkms.conf
  /usr/bin/kernelsu-loader             ksuinit userspace helper
  /usr/bin/load-kernelsu               module loading wrapper
  /etc/modprobe.d/00-kernelsu.conf     makes "ksu" an alias of "kernelsu"
  /lib/modules/<kver>/extra/kernelsu.ko  uncompressed copy for the loaders,
                                       published by the postinst per kernel

load-kernelsu uses the optional "modloader" utility when it is installed
and falls back to the bundled kernelsu-loader otherwise; both paths need
root privileges. Use "load-kernelsu --unload-first" to reload an already
loaded module.
